================================================================================================
(A) THE IDENTITY  P_v(t) = 2 |cos(pi(v t + tau_v))|,  v = 1..40
================================================================================================
  worst |LHS - RHS| = 7.216e-14  at (v,t) = (34, 0.75)
  NEGATIVE CONTROL with tau = 0 for every v: worst = 2.000e+00 (detects the wrong constant)

================================================================================================
(B) THE BASE CASE v = 2, which is the double-angle formula and needs no roots of unity
================================================================================================
  P_2(t) vs |2 sin 2pi t| : worst = 4.441e-16
  P_2(t) vs 2|cos(pi(2t+1/2))| : worst = 8.882e-16

================================================================================================
(C) MULTIPLICATIVITY  P_{ab}(t) = PROD_{i<a} P_b(t + i/(ab))
    -- the Finset reindexing k = i + a j; the step the Lean proof will live or die on
================================================================================================
  worst over 64 pairs (a,b) x 10 shifts : 7.816e-14  at (a,b,t) = (7, 7, 0.75)

================================================================================================
(D) THE tau BOOKKEEPING.  a tau_b + tau_a  ==  +- tau_{ab}  (mod 1) ?
================================================================================================
    a   b  a*tau_b + tau_a   tau_ab  mod-1 distance  ok
    3   5           0.0000     0.00       0.000e+00  True
    3   4           1.5000     0.50       0.000e+00  True
    4   3           0.5000     0.50       0.000e+00  True
    4   6           2.5000     0.50       0.000e+00  True
    5   7           0.0000     0.00       0.000e+00  True
    2   2           1.5000     0.50       0.000e+00  True
    6  10           3.5000     0.50       0.000e+00  True
    7   9           0.0000     0.00       0.000e+00  True
    8  12           4.5000     0.50       0.000e+00  True
  => the reduction closes in every parity case

================================================================================================
(E) THE FORM THE LEAN STATEMENT MUST TAKE
    multiplicative:  PROD_{k<v} |cos(pi(t+k/v))| <= 2^{1-v}     -- true everywhere
    additive:        (1/v) SUM -log|cos| >= (1-1/v) log 2       -- FALSE in Lean at a
                     pole, because Real.log 0 = 0 makes the vanishing term read 0
================================================================================================
  max over v<=40 of [PROD|cos| - 2^(1-v)] = 1.388e-16   (<= 0 up to a 1e-15 float floor)

  The additive counterexample must be evaluated EXACTLY, not in float: my first attempt
  computed cos(pi/2) numerically, got 6.1e-17 instead of 0, and so reported an additive
  mean of 18.67 -- the opposite of the point.  A demonstration that Real.log 0 = 0 breaks
  the bound cannot be run through a float cosine, because the input is never exactly 0.
  At v = 2, t = 1/2 the two cosines are cos(pi/2) = 0 and cos(pi) = -1, exactly:
    additive mean with Lean's convention = 0.000000,  floor claimed = 0.346574   -> FALSE, as predicted
    multiplicative form at the same point : 0.000000 <= 0.500000   -> True

================================================================================================
VERDICT
================================================================================================
  (A) identity                          : CONFIRMED
  (C) multiplicativity (the Lean step)  : CONFIRMED
  (D) tau composition                   : CONFIRMED
  negative control detects a wrong tau  : yes
  => THE REDUCTION IS SOUND.  Formalising lem:coset therefore splits into:
       1. v = 2, the double-angle formula      -- elementary, no roots of unity
       2. multiplicativity, k = i + a j        -- a Finset reindexing, checked here
       3. v an odd prime                       -- the only place roots of unity enter
     Steps 1 and 2 give every v that is a product of the primes handled in step 3.
